window.docContents[210].resolve({"/ISO___IEC-80000-___-The-Standards-Catalogue/The-references-layer-_LPAR_citation-metadata-only_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO___IEC-80000-___-The-Standards-Catalogue--The-references-layer-_LPAR_citation-metadata-only_RPAR_":{"contents":"\n\nA standard reference StandardRef is the bibliographic identity of one part: its\npublishing body (ISO or IEC), the series number (), the part number, the\nedition designation as printed, the publication date, and the part's English title.\nIt carries no normative content — only what is needed to cite the source. Its\ncite renders the conventional form, e.g. IEC 80000-6, Edition 2.0, 2022-11.\n\nRealized as structure StandardRef (Mathlib-free) with a StandardBody\n(ISO/IEC) field and the printed edition, date, and title as strings, plus\ndesignation (ISO 80000-3) and cite (the full reference). The values are read\noff each licensed part's cover page; nothing normative is transcribed.\n\nThe catalogue is the list of the thirteen catalogued parts in part order, each a\nstandard reference. It is the single place this work\nnames which editions of ISO/IEC 80000 the catalogued quantity-kinds and units are\ngrounded against, so the grounding is auditable rather than implicit.\n\nRealized as catalogue : List StandardRef, the thirteen definitions iso80000_1 …\niec80000_13 (with iec80000_6 and iec80000_13 the IEC-published parts). The\nedition is \"Second edition\" for every part except those two, both \"Edition 2.0\"; the\ndates are the cover dates of the licensed copies.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009ISO/IEC 80000 — The Standards Catalogue","header":"18.1. The references layer (citation metadata only)","id":"/ISO___IEC-80000-___-The-Standards-Catalogue/The-references-layer-_LPAR_citation-metadata-only_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO___IEC-80000-___-The-Standards-Catalogue--The-references-layer-_LPAR_citation-metadata-only_RPAR_"},"/The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_/Complex-valued-carriers/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_--Complex-valued-carriers":{"contents":"\n\n is the complexification of a carrier — the functor\n that, given the real arithmetic of , equips\n with the complex arithmetic. A complex-valued quantity is\n; the carrier varies\n( to prove, Float to run) while the kind  does not.This is a fourth point on the representation axis (R10): the three scalar carriers\n( to prove, a binary32 rounding spec to certify, Float to run) are all real,\nand  lifts each of them —  to prove,\n to certify rounding, \nto run. It is a Carrier, and a LawfulCarrier whenever  is (complex addition is\ncomponentwise, so the additivity laws lift for free); it carries a full field \nbut is unordered, so — unlike the real carriers — it sits outside the\nordinal/interval/ratio carrier tower: complex magnitudes are compared by modulus, not ranked.\n\nRealized as Complex R with the toolchain instances (, , , ,\nnegation, zero, one), a Carrier instance, and  / . Why parametric rather\nthan Mathlib's ? For the same reason the carrier is a parameter at all:\n is noncomputable (its parts are , which does not reduce — no\n-valued program runs or compiles, yet the executable carrier must be\nFloat-backed), and it is hardwired to  (there is no \" over\nFloat\"), whereas the representation axis needs complex over Float, over a binary32\nrounding spec, and over  from one definition. As with the reals — where\n is one instance of the carrier, not the universal one\n— Mathlib's  is the natural proof-carrier target: a ring isomorphism\n (its home the Dimension layer) transports\nany property of complex quantities at  to  and borrows its analysis\nlibrary, while the run side stays on .\n\nThe principal complex square root. The algebraic operations are total and\nsingle-valued, so they are plain instances; the square root is the exception — it has a\nbranch cut along the negative real axis. Selecting the principal branch needs exactly one\nsign decision (the sign of the imaginary part, with ),\nisolated as the one-field capability NegTest so the branch-free complex algebra needs\nonly the toolchain.\n\nRealized as Complex.sqrt:\n\nthe real and imaginary magnitudes two real square roots and the\nimaginary sign the one NegTest decision (Float deciding it computably, \nclassically). Complex is deliberately not made a full MathCarrier: a complex\n// suite would drag in a quadrant-aware angle and an unprovable\nlawfulness story the metrology does not need — the one transcendental the electromagnetic\nforward model uses is given directly and honestly as its own branch-tracked constructor.\n\nThe kind-tracked complex square root. The complex analogue of\nQuantity.sqrt, licensed by the same\nPowerKind : the complex square root of a (dimension-one)\nrelative permittivity is a (dimension-one) refractive index — a checked half-power crossing\nof kinds, now over a complex carrier. The complexification leaves the kind layer untouched,\nso this is the only new kind-level constructor the complex numbers require.\n\nRealized as Quantity.csqrt, the smart constructor wrapping the\nprincipal complex square root under the same PowerKind (1/2) certificate as the real\nhalf-power; named csqrt because the complex root is a genuinely distinct operation (it\nhas a branch cut) even though its kind discipline is identical.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009The Function Calculus and Complex-Valued Carriers (R12)","header":"12.2. Complex-valued carriers","id":"/The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_/Complex-valued-carriers/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Function-Calculus-and-Complex-Valued-Carriers-_LPAR_R12_RPAR_--Complex-valued-carriers"},"/The-Proved-Spine/Scale-___-the-operator-based-division-of-_LANGLE_property_RANGLE_-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-12_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Proved-Spine--Scale-___-the-operator-based-division-of-_LANGLE_property_RANGLE_-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-12_RPAR_":{"contents":"\n\nThe four scale types of Dybkær Fig. 12.21, ordered by operator richness:\n, admitting respectively , then\n, then , then .\n\nA four-constructor inductive with a rank : ScaleType → Nat and the induced\n≤. The lemmas le_def, le_refl, le_trans, and le_antisymm establish that\n≤ is the expected linear order.\n\nThe scale order is a linear order: reflexive (le_refl), transitive\n(le_trans), and antisymmetric (le_antisymm) on the scale types.\n\nReflexivity and transitivity are inherited from Nat.le on rank.\nAntisymmetry reduces to Nat.le_antisymm on the ranks followed by a four-by-four\ncase split (cases a <;> cases b <;> simp_all).\n\nOperator-availability monotonicity. A richer scale licenses every operation a\npoorer one does: if  then AllowsOrder, AllowsDifference, and\nAllowsRatio each transport from  to . This is a meta-theorem OWL2's\nreasoner cannot even phrase, since it quantifies over which operations are\ndefined for a kind. Builds on the scale types.\n\nDecide all sixteen ordered pairs: cases a <;> cases b <;> simp_all [AllowsOrder,\nAllowsDifference, AllowsRatio, le_def, rank].\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009The Proved Spine","header":"5.1. Scale — the operator-based division of ⟨property⟩ (Dybkær Ch. 12)","id":"/The-Proved-Spine/Scale-___-the-operator-based-division-of-_LANGLE_property_RANGLE_-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-12_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Proved-Spine--Scale-___-the-operator-based-division-of-_LANGLE_property_RANGLE_-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-12_RPAR_"}});